WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.330
Push an empty bicycle hard and let go: for a few seconds it carries on with nobody riding it, and when it leans to one side the handlebar turns by itself into the fall and the bicycle straightens up.

2
00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:15.300
The reason is not the gyroscopic effect you were taught at school.

3
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:22.310
Because physicists built a bicycle that cancelled both the gyroscope and the caster trail — and it did not fall over either.

4
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.960
Push an empty bicycle down a straight road and let it go.

5
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.050
For a few seconds it runs upright, far longer than it has any right to.

6
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:49.620
Then it leans a little to one side, and at that exact moment the handlebar turns itself the same way, the bicycle traces a shallow arc, and it stands back up.

7
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.110
No one is touching it, no one is correcting it.

8
00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:57.560
There is nobody on the saddle, no hand on the bars.

9
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.210
And still the machine gathers itself together.

10
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:12.220
The first thing you feel when you watch this pushes you toward an explanation that is not true: the wheels are spinning, a spinning wheel is a gyroscope, and a gyroscope resists being tipped over.

11
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.350
The image of the spinning top we have known since childhood takes over.

12
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:20.160
A top stands while it spins and lies down when it stops.

13
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.690
The bicycle must be the same.

14
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:26.820
But the detail that matters in that scene is not the turning of the wheel.

15
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.670
It is the handlebar turning by itself.

16
00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.640
What keeps a bicycle upright is not resistance but correction.

17
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.050
And that correction is nothing more than a single movement: steering toward the side it is falling.

18
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.540
To see why, you first have to accept something.

19
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.190
A two-wheeled vehicle cannot stand still and stay up.

20
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:50.320
The two points where it touches the ground form a line segment.

21
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.050
Along that line there is a base of support that amounts to a tire print a few centimeters wide.

22
00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:02.580
If the center of gravity is not directly above that line, gravity begins to tip the bicycle sideways.

23
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:09.590
Whatever you do when you hold a broom by its handle and try to keep it upright on your palm, the bicycle has to do as well.

24
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:17.240
You slide your hand toward the side the broom is falling, you bring the support point back under the center of gravity, and the broom straightens.

25
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.730
Then it starts to fall again, and you slide again.

26
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:26.780
Standing upright is not a state; it is a continuously repeated sequence of corrections.

27
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.430
The bicycle's palm is the print the wheels leave on the ground.

28
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.560
And the only way to slide that print sideways is to turn the steering.

29
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:47.330
If the bicycle starts leaning right and the front wheel turns right, the bicycle sweeps an arc to the right; along that arc the contact points slide to the right of the center of gravity and the frame comes back up.

30
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.660
The fall is not being resisted.

31
00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.990
The machine is moving in underneath it.

32
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:57.320
Everyone who rides does this without noticing.

33
00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:02.250
In fact, most people do it in a way they will stubbornly deny.

34
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.500
When you want to turn left, you first flick the handlebar to the right for a very short moment.

35
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.630
That opposite move slides the bottom of the bicycle to the right, the body leans left, and you carry that lean through by steering left.

36
00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:25.360
This is called countersteering, and above about ten kilometers an hour it is what unavoidably happens in every turn, on a motorcycle as much as on a bicycle.

37
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:33.170
Ask someone which way they turn the bars when they turn left, and the answer you get will not match the answer a sensor fitted to those bars would give.

38
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.140
The body has learned something the mind never did.

39
00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.110
But a ridden bicycle is the easy part of the problem.

40
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:46.040
The hard part is how the empty one, pushed and released, manages it.

41
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.050
Because there is no brain there to decide.

42
00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:54.300
The bicycle itself has to be a machine that steers toward the side it leans.

43
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.950
That is, a device with a feedback loop built into it.

44
00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.800
So what builds that loop?

45
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.090
For more than a century there were two standard answers to that question.

46
00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:15.620
The first was the gyroscopic effect of the spinning front wheel: when the bicycle leans right, the angular momentum of the front wheel produces a reaction in the direction of turning the bars to the right.

47
00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:20.070
The second was the geometric property we call trail.

48
00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:32.040
The imaginary steering axis extended by the front fork strikes the ground at a point; on a normal bicycle, the point where the front wheel touches the ground lies a few centimeters behind that point.

49
00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.170
The same geometry is what straightens the casters of a shopping cart.

50
00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:44.620
The front wheel behaves like a wheel dragged along behind, and when the bicycle leans, the reaction from the ground again turns the bars toward the lean.

51
00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.310
Both explanations make sense.

52
00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.000
Both really do work.

53
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.450
And both, taken separately, are unnecessary.

54
00:04:54.450 --> 00:05:00.340
Saying that was not easy, because there was a serious mathematical tradition on the other side.

55
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.750
The equations of motion for a bicycle had been written at the end of the nineteenth century.

56
00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:17.560
Emmanuel Carvallo in eighteen ninety-seven and Francis Whipple in eighteen ninety-nine, independently of one another, set down the sets of equations describing the leaning and steering motion of a two-wheeled vehicle.

57
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:27.930
At the beginning of the twentieth century, names such as Felix Klein and Fritz Noether placed the question inside gyroscope theory, and the sentence that entered the textbooks was this:

58
00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.540
what holds a bicycle up is the gyroscopic effect of the spinning wheel.

59
00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:38.590
The first person to shake that sentence seriously was a researcher and chemist named David Jones.

60
00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:46.560
In a now-famous article published in a physics magazine in nineteen seventy, he described his attempt to build a bicycle that could not be ridden.

61
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:55.490
His aim was plain: if I cancel the gyroscopic effect, the bicycle should become unrideable, and that way I will have proved the theory by experiment.

62
00:05:55.490 --> 00:06:03.300
He mounted a second wheel on the front fork, next to the real front wheel: one held up off the ground, spinning the other way.

63
00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:10.150
The angular momenta of the two wheels cancelled each other out, so the gyroscopic effect at the front was close to zero.

64
00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.800
Jones got on the bicycle and rode it comfortably.

65
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.450
He spun the counter-wheel faster; he still rode it.

66
00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:20.940
He spun it in the same direction; he still rode it.

67
00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.950
The unrideable bicycle could be ridden.

68
00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:29.520
What Jones found to be the genuinely sensitive setting was not the gyroscope but the trail.

69
00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:40.210
When he moved the front wheel roughly ten centimeters forward of its normal position and reversed the trail, what came out was a truly ill-tempered machine: pushed and released, it fell over at once.

70
00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.700
So trail looked more decisive than the gyroscope.

71
00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.390
This rescued half of the explanation.

72
00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:50.840
What keeps a bicycle upright is not the gyroscope but the geometry.

73
00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.650
Forty years later, that half was questioned too.

74
00:06:54.650 --> 00:07:04.060
In April of two thousand eleven, an article in the journal Science described a trap set by a team from Delft University of Technology and Cornell University.

75
00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:15.870
The group, made up of Jodi Kooijman, Arend Schwab, Andy Ruina, Jim Papadopoulos and Jaap Meijaard, first built a theoretical model on paper: two point masses and, in place of wheels,

76
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:21.280
two contacts that behaved like ice skate blades with no rotational inertia at all.

77
00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:27.970
The single purpose of this model was to zero out the contribution of gyroscope and trail by definition.

78
00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:31.940
The model came out self-stable above a certain speed.

79
00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.270
The real task was making it out of metal.

80
00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:45.640
The machine they built did not look much like a bicycle: small wheels, a pair of counter-rotating wheels mounted above each of them, two heavy masses sitting out in the open.

81
00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:52.810
The counter-rotating wheels cancelled the angular momentum of the front and the rear, so the gyroscopic effect was gone.

82
00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.140
The trail was measured at minus four millimeters.

83
00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:04.110
The point where the front wheel touched the ground was not behind the point where the steering axis met the ground, but four millimeters in front of it.

84
00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:09.840
Neither of the two ingredients that classical theory demanded for a bicycle that stands up was present.

85
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:16.370
This machine was pushed, was knocked out of balance with a kick from the side, and it gathered itself back together.

86
00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:27.380
Above a certain threshold speed, about two point three meters per second, close to eight kilometers an hour, it would wobble its way back to running straight and upright after a disturbance from outside.

87
00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:32.790
The lean and steer motion that was measured matched the curve the theoretical model had predicted.

88
00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.680
So what was left?

89
00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.570
Mass distribution.

90
00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:45.180
A bicycle's steering assembly, meaning the fork, the bars and the front wheel, can turn freely as a single unit around the steering axis.

91
00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:50.110
The center of gravity of that unit does not have to sit directly on the axis.

92
00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:56.000
What is critical is that the center of gravity of the front assembly sits low and ahead of that axis.

93
00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:07.010
In such an arrangement, the moment the bicycle leans right, gravity does not only try to tip the frame over; it also tries to turn the hanging front assembly to the right around the steering axis.

94
00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:11.940
The front assembly is light, and it responds faster than the frame does.

95
00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.830
While the fall is still a lean of only a few degrees, the steering has already turned the right way.

96
00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:21.960
This means the bicycle is using its own fall in its own favor.

97
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:25.450
The falling is itself the signal that triggers the correction.

98
00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:38.380
And that was exactly the conclusion the team underlined in the article: almost any self-stable bicycle can be made unstable by spoiling its trail alone, or by changing the gyroscopic effect of the front wheel alone,

99
00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:42.510
or by shifting the center of gravity of the front assembly alone.

100
00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.880
All three are effective.

101
00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.050
None of the three is necessary on its own.

102
00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:55.060
Stability is not a property owned by any one of them; it is a condition in which the account between them comes out even.

103
00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:59.830
At this point what a person wants is obvious: a one-sentence answer.

104
00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:02.840
Why does a bicycle not fall over?

105
00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:08.090
We now know what the answer is not, but there is no sentence that clean to put in its place.

106
00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:11.260
The reason for that is not ignorance.

107
00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:14.270
It is the structure of the equations.

108
00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:24.960
The model the Delft and Cornell team published in two thousand seven, used today as a common point of comparison in the field, describes a bicycle with twenty-five physical variables.

109
00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:35.010
Wheel radii, the tilt of the steering axis, trail, wheelbase, the masses of the rear frame and the front assembly, the positions of the centers of those masses,

110
00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:38.820
and the moments of inertia of each of them about three axes.

111
00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:43.110
On top of all that come gravity and speed.

112
00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:50.440
Stability emerges from a particular combination of these twenty-five numbers; the value of any one of them on its own tells you nothing.

113
00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:58.570
You cannot learn a bicycle's trail and say whether it will fall over, just as you cannot know how a dish tastes by looking only at how much salt is in it.

114
00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:03.660
When the equations are solved, two characteristic forms of motion appear.

115
00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:11.470
The first is called weave: the leaning and steering motions of the bicycle oscillating together in a rocking that repeats a few times a second.

116
00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:17.680
At low speeds this oscillation grows and topples the bicycle; above a certain speed it dies away.

117
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:25.330
The second is called capsize: the bicycle, without oscillating, curling slowly over to one side in a lean that keeps increasing.

118
00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:29.460
This motion is damped at low speeds and grows slowly at high speeds.

119
00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:33.590
For the benchmark model, the calculated values are these.

120
00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:39.960
The weave motion is damped above four point three meters per second, roughly fifteen kilometers an hour.

121
00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:46.970
The capsize motion begins to grow slowly above six meters per second, somewhere around twenty-one kilometers an hour.

122
00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:53.020
That narrow band between the two thresholds is the range in which a bicycle is genuinely self-stable.

123
00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:58.750
Below it and above it, a bicycle left to its own devices ends up on the ground sooner or later.

124
00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:03.680
That is why the empty bicycle you push runs for a few seconds and then lies down.

125
00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:09.250
Friction slows it, the speed drops below that band, and the balance disappears.

126
00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:13.540
There is a conclusion that follows from this and is often told wrongly.

127
00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:17.990
Self-stability is not a requirement for rideability.

128
00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:28.040
A bicycle that begins to lean over at high speed is not dangerous at all, because that motion grows very slowly and the tiniest correction the rider gives the bars suppresses it.

129
00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:36.810
In the same way, a person can perfectly well ride a bicycle that topples the instant it is left alone; the rider fills in for the feedback loop the machine is missing.

130
00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:42.380
On a ridden bicycle, the real controller providing the balance is always the human being.

131
00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:47.630
The machine's own stability is no more than a gift that makes that person's work easier.

132
00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:49.680
And there is this too.

133
00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:57.810
The sentence going around on the internet, that science still cannot explain how a bicycle stays up, is not true as it stands.

134
00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:06.100
The equations have been in our hands since the end of the nineteenth century, they were verified in two thousand seven by methods independent of one another,

135
00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:10.710
and they have been shown many times over to agree with experimental measurements.

136
00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:17.240
Give us the dimensions of a bicycle and we can tell you with numbers at which speeds that bicycle will be self-stable.

137
00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:26.810
More than that, as happened in two thousand eleven, we can calculate a strange machine designed on paper in advance, then build it out of metal and confirm it.

138
00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:30.140
What cannot be explained is not the physics.

139
00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:35.550
What cannot be explained is the short sentence that translates this physics into intuition.

140
00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:39.200
What is missing is not the knowledge but the story.

141
00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:43.010
That distinction touches something wider than the bicycle.

142
00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:49.060
The measure of whether we have understood a phenomenon is usually taken to be whether we can describe it briefly.

143
00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:53.990
But nature does not always package its explanations into single-cause sentences.

144
00:13:53.990 --> 00:14:01.160
In some systems, the essential thing is a property found in none of the parts, one that lives only in the ratios between them.

145
00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.690
The bicycle is like this.

146
00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:09.260
There is no such object as a self-stable bicycle; there is a self-stable setting.

147
00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:16.590
When geometry, mass distribution and speed come together in the right way, falling turns into straightening.

148
00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:26.800
And we found this setting some seventy years before the equations were written, not from a calculated design but from a craft that came out of the hands of makers who patiently tried and failed.

149
00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:37.010
The people in the bicycle workshops of the nineteenth century who fiddled with fork angle, trail distance and wheel size may have worked out what did the job, but they did not know why it did.

150
00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:46.900
Today we know the mathematics underneath it, and that has a concrete payoff: we no longer have to hunt for stability by trial and error, we can design it by calculation.

151
00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:56.950
The same equations are used in setting motorcycle geometry, in two-wheeled robots that keep their own balance, and in sizing custom bicycles for riders with disabilities.

152
00:14:56.950 --> 00:15:05.400
An ordinary object everyone assumed they understood for a century turned out, once it was opened up, to still be a machine worth working on.

153
00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:09.050
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

154
00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:12.700
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
